ich are shown/obvious. Actually, \"certain\" — unitsdb IDs are like \"http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/...\"? The instruction example: \"u:degree_Celsius\". I'll use u:degree_Celsius for °C, u:volt for V, u:bar for bar, u:hour? m³/h is \"u:m3_per_hour\"? Not sure. I'll keep null for all except degree_Celsius and volt. Hmm, even bar — \"u:bar\" seems obvious. But to be safe and consistent, maybe just leave all null? The rule says leave null unless certain. u:degree_Celsius is given verbatim in the instructions, so that one I'm certain about. u:volt — pattern suggests u:volt. I'll include u:volt and u:degree_Celsius, rest null. Actually, let me just be conservative and leave all null except u:degree_Celsius (explicitly shown in schema example). Hmm, but that seems overly cautious. The example in rule 3: \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\". That's the canonical form. For volt, I'd guess \"u:volt\". I'll include u:volt too. For bar → \"u:bar\". mm → \"u:millimetre\"? QUDT uses \"MilliM\". Not certain → null. m³/h → null. m³ → null. % → null (or \"u:percent\"? QUDT \"PERCENT\" — not certain) → null. years → null.\n\nOK let me also decide config_level entries:\n\nconfig_level:\n1. {\n   \"attribute\": \"water_temperature_range\",\n   \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\",\n   \"axis\": \"meter_temperature_class\",\n   \"values\": [\n     {\"condition\": \"T30\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 30}},\n     {\"condition\": \"T50\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}},\n     {\"condition\": \"T30/70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 30, \"max\": 70}},\n     {\"condition\": \"T70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 70}}\n   ]\n }\n Wait — the schema for config_level values: \"values[]: {condition, value}\" and rule 5 says values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So unit_symbol can be on each value or at attribute level. Schema shows config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. Rule 5 says {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll put unit_symbol on each