n- overload_flowrate_q4\n- ratio_q3_q1\n- ratio_q2_q1 (type_level)\n- pressure_loss_delta_p\n- connection_thread\n- construction_length_l → \"construction_length_l\" or \"construction_length\". I'll use \"construction_length_l\" to mirror the label. Hmm, rule: canonical labels; \"Construction length L\" → construction_length_l. OK.\n- water_temperature_range\n- meter_temperature_class\n- maximum_working_pressure\n- maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄)\n- maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone (Q₁ ≤ Q < Q₂)\n- scale_interval\n- scale_interval_testing_mode\n- calculator_capacity\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode\n- accuracy_class\n- mechanical_class\n- climatic_class\n- electromagnetic_class\n- environmental_classification\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class\n- battery_type, battery_voltage, battery_lifetime\n- installation_position\n\ntype_level values shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers[]}. I'll include footnote_markers: [] or omit? Rule 2 shape includes footnote_markers. I'll include empty array or omit when none. To be concise, omit when empty. Hmm, \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — includes footnote_markers. I'll include footnote_markers: [] everywhere? That's verbose but compliant. The examples in the prompt show footnote_markers only when present (e.g., model_level example values lack footnote_markers). I'll omit when empty, matching examples.\n\nFor string-valued type_level (installation_position, classes), value is string with unit_symbol null. E.g., {\"value\": \"M1\", \"unit_symbol\": null}. I'll include unit_symbol null.\n\nLet me now also decide: should temperature classes themselves be type_level as a list? The four classes are config options, captured as config_level conditions. I'll add type_level \"meter_temperature_classes\" maybe? No — config_level axis covers it. Skip.\n\nBattery: single cell \"li-battery 3,6 V, life time 16 years\". Split into battery_type \"Li battery\", battery_voltage 3